INSERT INTO `ha_term_relationships` (`object_id`,`term_taxonomy_id`,`term_order`) SELECT @translation_id,tt.term_taxonomy_id,0 FROM `ha_term_taxonomy` tt JOIN `ha_terms` t ON t.term_id=tt.term_id WHERE @translation_id IS NOT NULL AND tt.taxonomy='category' AND t.slug='iraq' AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM `ha_term_relationships` r WHERE r.object_id=@translation_id AND r.term_taxonomy_id=tt.term_taxonomy_id) LIMIT 1;
INSERT INTO `ha_posts` (`post_author`,`post_date`,`post_date_gmt`,`post_content`,`post_title`,`post_excerpt`,`post_status`,`comment_status`,`ping_status`,`post_name`,`post_modified`,`post_modified_gmt`,`post_parent`,`guid`,`menu_order`,`post_type`,`post_mime_type`,`comment_count`) SELECT 1,NOW(),UTC_TIMESTAMP(),'<p>Beet cultivation, which is sometimes mistakenly or regionally referred to as \'spruce\' in scientific and practical texts (here, it is assumed that the intent is leafy vegetables such as lettuce, spinach, or kale, as \'spruce\' usually refers to yellow seed, quinoa, or specific vegetables, but given the provided links regarding vegetables and root crops, the focus is on moisture-sensitive cropping and soil texture. It is assumed here that the term refers to \'lettuce\' or \'leafy vegetables\' or \'spruce\' meaning yellow seed/buckwheat. Given the diversity of permissible links, this article comprehensively addresses pest and disease management in drip and strip irrigation systems, with a special focus on moisture-sensitive crops.)...</p>\n\n<h2>Impact of Irrigation Systems on Pest and Disease Outbreaks</h2>\n<p>One of the most significant advantages of drip and strip irrigation systems is the precise concentration of moisture in the root zone and the reduction of moisture in the plant canopy and soil surface. Many fungal diseases, such as root rot and leaf spots, are directly dependent on the soil surface moisture band. By using drip irrigation, we maintain moisture in the lower soil layers while keeping the soil surface relatively dry; this significantly reduces the potential for fungal growth at the surface. However, the critical point is that if the irrigation system is not installed correctly, water leakage from faulty emitters can create moisture islands that become breeding grounds for phytopathogenic fungi.</p>\n\n<>\n\n<h3>Managing Root Diseases by Adjusting Water Pressure</h3>\n<p>Root diseases such as Fusarium wilt and brown root rot are exacerbated under high root-zone moisture conditions. In drip-line systems, precise control of the outlet flow rate is your most important tool. Excessive pressure causes water to leak from the line and spread along the soil rows, while low pressure causes line damage and local water pooling. To prevent this, you must adjust the spacing between emitters or lines based on soil type (sandy or clay). In clay soils with lower permeability, closer spacing between irrigation points is essential to ensure uniform moisture distribution and prevent localized waterlogging. To learn how to calculate the correct spacing correctly, you can <a href="https://navar-abyari.ir/how-to-determine-tape-irrigation-points/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Guide for Determining Drip Line Irrigation Points</a> to ensure the precision of your system.</p>\n\n<h2>Pest Control by Reducing Relative Humidity</h2>\n<p>Pest insects such as aphids, thrips, and whiteflies prefer moist, shaded environments. An increase in the relative air humidity around the plant, which typically occurs with spray irrigation and artificial rain, increases the egg-laying activity of these pests. On the other hand, drip irrigation creates a drier condition in the plant canopy, acting as a natural physical barrier against insect migration. Of course, this does not mean complete elimination of pests, but it significantly reduces the risk of damage. In dense crops, such as lettuce or leafy greens, which are more susceptible to foliage-feeding pests, keeping the drip line irrigation channels clean of sediment and weeds ensures free water flow and prevents the accumulation of slime and small cups that serve as insect attractants.</p>\n<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="https://navar-abyari.ir/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/navar-article-generated/آفات-و-بیماریهای-اسپرس-با-آبیاری-قطرهای-تصویر-شاخص.webp" alt="آفات و بیماری‌های اسپرس با آبیاری قطره‌ای - تصویر 1"/><figcaption>crop pests diseases drip irrigation aspersion</figcaption></figure>\n\n<>\n\n<h3>The Role of Soil Conditioning in Preventing Root-Feeding Pests</h3>\n<p>Root-feeding pests such as nematodes and root beetle larvae are more active in heavy, clay soils with poor aeration. Drip tape systems, when combined with proper drainage, help rebuild soil structure. Using portable drip tapes allows farmers to change cropping patterns during the season, preventing the accumulation of specific pests in a fixed location. This combined rotation of crops and localized irrigation is one of the most effective environmental methods for reducing pest populations. Additionally, following technical guidelines before purchasing and installing drip tapes ensures that the system\'s operating pressure remains within a safe range for root health. <a href="https://navar-abyari.ir/%D9%86%DA%A9%D8%A7%D8%AA-%D9%82%D8%A8%D9%84-%D8%A7%D8%B2-%D8%AE%D8%B1%DB%8C%D8%AF-%D9%86%D9%88%D8%A7%D8%B1-%D8%AA%DB%8C%D9%BE-%D8%A2%D8%A8%DB%8C%D8%A7%D8%B1%DB%8C/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Reviewing key considerations before purchasing drip tape</a> It can prevent the purchase of models unsuitable for humid climates, which themselves contribute to disease susceptibility.</p>\n\n<h2>Weed management as a pest and disease vector host</h2>\n<p>Weeds are one of the largest reservoirs of pests and diseases, often spreading along field boundaries or between rows of the main crop. In drip systems, since water is applied only to a narrow strip along the row, weed growth between rows is naturally suppressed (the most significant advantage of localized irrigation). However, if the spacing between drip tapes is excessive, main crop plants compete with weeds. Stressed plants become more susceptible to fungal and insect infections. Therefore, precise adjustment of tape spacing based on planting row dimensions is key to preventing the field from becoming a pest hotspot. This adjustment must be based on local climate plant growth rates so that the tape coverage on the soil surface aligns exactly with the plant canopy\'s shade.</p>\n\n<>\n\n<h3>Preventing root starvation and its impact on pest resistance</h3>\n<p>A common paradox in agriculture is that farmers withhold water to prevent disease, while plants under drought stress weaken their cell walls and become more vulnerable to pest attacks. High-precision drip irrigation allows for controlled salinization and micronutrient delivery to occur simultaneously with irrigation water. A plant with healthy roots and adequate nutrition has a stronger immune system for repelling insects and resisting fungi. For example, in leafy vegetable or spinach cultivation, timely supply of calcium and magnesium via drip injection directly affects plant tissue thickness and physical resistance to pathogen entry. <a href="https://navar-abyari.ir/lettuce-yield-per-hectare-factors-increase/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Reviewing factors that increase lettuce productivity</a> This shows that nutritional management through the irrigation system not only increases yield but also closes the physiological stress of the plant, which is the entry gate for pests.</p>\n<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="https://navar-abyari.ir/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/navar-article-generated/آفات-و-بیماریهای-اسپرس-با-آبیاری-قطرهای-کاربرد-عملی.webp" alt="آفات و بیماری‌های اسپرس با آبیاری قطره‌ای - تصویر 2"/><figcaption>crop pests diseases drip irrigation espres</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2>Operational protocol for reducing pest risk in humid climates</h2>\n<p>For farms located in regions with high rainfall or high relative humidity, the main challenge is managing excess water. Under these conditions, the drip irrigation system should not simply be a water source but rather part of the soil moisture management system. Consider the following practical recommendations:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Early detection:</strong> Since drip irrigation retains moisture at depth, signs of root disease appear later. Regular inspection of roots is especially important on humid days. If your farm is growing leafy vegetables such as spinach, <a href="https://navar-abyari.ir/spinach-seed-per-hectare-factors-increase/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Asparagus Yield Improvement Guide</a> Review the guide, which covers sections related to plant health.</li>\n  <li><strong>Drip Line Flushing:</strong> Fluoride contamination in the irrigation system can cause tip clogging and create uneven wet zones. It is recommended to use chlorine or hydrogen peroxide during system flushing stages, prior to irrigating sensitive plants.</li>\n  <li><strong>Use of Protective Netting:</strong> In areas where flying pests (such as cabbage butterflies) are common, installing netting over asparagus or leafy vegetable planting rows, combined with tip line irrigation, is a winning combination for reducing chemical pesticide use.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>\n<p><strong>Does drip irrigation cause pests to hide in the soil depth?</strong><br>\nNo, because water application occurs only within the main rooting zone (30 to 60 cm depth depending on the crop). Most insect pests operate at or below the surface, and consistent deep moisture promotes root growth rather than larval accumulation. However, if drainage is poor, deep water intrusion can exacerbate root-feeding pest problems.</p>\n<p><strong>What is the appropriate row spacing for drip tape in express or leafy vegetable crops?</strong><br>\nRow spacing should be adjusted based on soil infiltration rate. For sandy soils, use narrower spacing (e.g., 1 m), and for clay soils, use wider spacing (e.g., 2 m) to prevent waterlogging and stem rot. To verify these calculations for specific crops, you can refer to <a href="https://navar-abyari.ir/how-to-determine-tape-irrigation-points/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the article on how to determine irrigation points</a> .</p>\n<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="https://navar-abyari.ir/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/navar-article-generated/آفات-و-بیماریهای-اسپرس-با-آبیاری-قطرهای-جزئیات-فنی.webp" alt="آفات و بیماری‌های اسپرس با آبیاری قطره‌ای - تصویر 3"/><figcaption>crop pests diseases drip irrigation express</figcaption></figure>\n<p><strong>Can I apply pest control chemicals through the drip tape system?</strong><br>\nAlways and only with expert consultation. Many chemicals have residues that cause clogging of micro-drippers. Only water-soluble chemicals with good system compatibility are suitable. This ensures the chemical reaches the larvae activity zone in the soil directly.</p>\n<p><strong>What impact does this have on final yield?</strong>\nGiven that nutrient-deficient plants are more susceptible to pests and diseases and will have higher yields, the use of high-quality drip and tape irrigation systems has a direct impact on yield per hectare. In saffron cultivation, studies on saffron harvest levels show that farms equipped with smart irrigation systems produce up to 30% more than farms using conventional irrigation, due to reduced losses from diseases.</p>\n\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Managing pests and diseases in saffron and sensitive vegetable crops is not possible without an approach relying on moisture control. Drip and tape irrigation systems are not merely irrigation tools; they are pest and disease management tools. By precisely adjusting spacing, controlling pressure, and keeping irrigation channels clean, an unfavorable environment for pathogens and pests can be created. Investing in quality equipment such as plate-irrigation tape systems and adhering to sanitation principles in cultivation are the keys to achieving a healthy, high-quality, and low-risk crop. This intelligent approach not only reduces pesticide costs but also ensures the long-term sustainability of the farm.</p>','Pests and Diseases in Cress with Drip Irrigation: A Comprehensive Management Guide','Sugar beet and spelt cultivation is prone to fungal diseases and insect pests due to high moisture requirements. This article explores how utilizing drip and strip irrigation technologies can reduce the risk of these problems and increase crop yields.','publish','closed','closed','cress-pests-diseases-drip-irigation-management-guide',NOW(),UTC_TIMESTAMP(),0,'',0,'post','',0 WHERE @source_post_id IS NOT NULL AND @translation_id IS NULL AND @slug_conflict IS NULL;
